>
  <h2>The problem with a wood frame</h2>
  <p>Composite and PVC deck boards carry warranties of 25 years or more. A pressure-treated wood frame often can't keep up. Water sits on the joists, the wood dries, crowns and twists, and over time the frame rots from the top down. In Utah, freeze-thaw cycles speed that up.</p>

  <Photo src="/images/ideas/steel-frame-second-story-deck" alt="Steel-framed second-story deck built by TC Decks" />

  <h2>Why steel is better</h2>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li><strong>Dead flat.</strong> Steel joists are made straight and stay straight, for a flatter deck and cleaner board lines.</li>
    <li><strong>Won't rot.</strong> Water and insects can't break down steel.</li>
    <li><strong>Longer spans.</strong> Fewer posts and footings, more open space under a second-story deck.</li>
    <li><strong>Doesn't burn.</strong> Worth thinking about near fire pits, outdoor kitchens and wildland areas.</li>
    <li><strong>Matches your boards.</strong> A steel frame under TimberTech or Trex gives you a deck where every part is built for the long haul.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where we recommend it most</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Second-story decks</strong>, where long spans and a clean underside matter.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/hot-tub-decks">Hot tub decks</a></strong>, which need a stiff, strong frame.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="/covered-decks">Covered decks</a></strong> and decks with a dry patio or finished ceiling below.</li>
    <li><strong>Hillside lots</strong> around Traverse Mountain, Alpine and the benches, where posts get tall.</li>
  </ul>

  <Photo src="/images/ideas/steel-frame-deck-stairs" alt="Steel-framed deck and stairs" />

  <h2>Steel, OC Lumber or wood?</h2>
  <p>Steel isn't the only rot-proof option. We are also on the Owens Corning <a href="/oc-lumber">OC Lumber Pro Build Team</a>, a wood-free, rust-free framing material. And when a project uses wood, we protect every joist with <a href="/blog/protecting-your-deck-with-g-tape-the-tc-decks-way-to-longevity">flashing tape</a>. On a phone estimate we'll tell you which fits your deck and budget.</p>
  <p>Good framing also needs good footings. We often set steel frames on <a href="/blog/rethinking-deck-foundations-ground-screws-the-modern-alternative-to-concrete-footings">ground screws</a>, which go in fast and give a measured, solid base. For the full story, read <a href="/blog/steel-deck-framing-fortress-evolution">why we build on Fortress Evolution</a>.</p>
  <p>Want a deck that's built to outlast the boards? <a href="/contact">Request a free estimate</a>.</p>
</Service>
